# Git workflow (always apply)

**One branch and one PR per milestone; small atomic commits.** Full detail: `docs/design/11-engineering-standards.md` section 12.

## Branches
- Never commit to `main`. Before the first edit of a milestone, branch from an up-to-date `main`: `<type>/<milestone>-<slug>`, e.g. `feat/m1-domain-models`, `feat/m4-sim-kernel`. Types: `feat fix refactor perf test docs build ci chore`.
- **One milestone = one branch = one pull request.** The slice list in `docs/design/06-milestones.md` is the **commit plan** for that branch. Do **not** open one PR per slice and do **not** stack PRs, unless the user asks. Work outside a milestone (a fix, a docs tweak) gets its own small branch and PR.
- Keep the branch current by merging or rebasing `main` into it. Never delete a branch that another open PR is based on.

## Commits (this is what keeps a big PR reviewable)
- **Atomic:** one logical change per commit; every commit builds and passes `uv run check` (bisectable). Tests go in the same commit as the code they cover. Generated/golden updates are their own commit (`chore(golden): ...`). Refactors, formatting and dependency bumps never share a commit with behaviour changes.
- Target **<= 400 changed lines per commit** (lockfiles, generated schemas, goldens and data dumps excluded). The owner reviews the PR commit by commit, so each commit must make sense on its own.
- **Conventional Commits:** `type(scope): imperative summary` (<= 72 chars), blank line, body explaining **why** (and notable trade-offs), footer with the changelog fragment id and any `BREAKING CHANGE:`. No WIP/fixup noise in the history sent for review; tidy before requesting review.
- Commit as you go, in small steps, rather than one big commit at the end.

## Human review checkpoint (mandatory before any push or PR)
**No PR is opened, and no branch is pushed, until the user has reviewed the work and said so.** At the end of each milestone:
1. All slices are committed locally; `uv run check --tier pr` is green; the milestone report (`docs/milestones/Mx.md`) and `docs/status.md` are updated.
2. **Stop and present a review package**: branch name, commits grouped by slice, `git diff --stat main...HEAD`, what/why per slice, how it was verified, impact flags (SIM_VERSION/SCHEMA_VERSION/config/migration/perf), deviations, anything uncertain. Offer to show the diff pane.
3. **Wait** for an explicit go-ahead ("open the PR", "looks good, push it"). Silence, a thumbs-up on an earlier milestone, or a previous approval of different work is not approval. A request for an interim look at the local branch is a preview, not approval to push. Requested changes go in new atomic commits (or a tidy-up of unpushed history) and are re-presented.
4. Only then: push the branch and open the **one** PR (`gh pr create`, template filled in, one section per slice). The user may still review again on GitHub before merging.

## Pull request
- Title = a Conventional Commit line naming the milestone. Description (template `.github/pull_request_template.md`): **Summary**, **Changes** (per slice), **How verified**, **Impact** (sim_version / schema / config / migration / performance), **Docs and changelog** (fragment ids), **Follow-ups**, **Checklist** (clean-code review list, section 10).
- Open it only when `uv run check --tier pr` is green locally. Link the relevant design sections. `uv run pr-size` reports the branch size and is informational for milestone PRs.
- The user merges (merge commits so far) and may delete the branch afterwards.

## What agents may and may not do
- May: create the milestone branch and make atomic commits locally; after the **human review checkpoint** is approved, push the branch and open one PR (with `gh`).
- May not: push or open a PR before the user's review; open per-slice or stacked PRs; commit to or push `main`; merge a PR unless the user says so; force-push shared branches (use `--force-with-lease` only on your own branch); skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing; rewrite published history; delete remote branches you did not create.
- The remote is `https://github.com/AB-Law/FootyStreams` (public). `gh` is authenticated as the owner, so every outward action needs the checkpoint above (the owner also cannot approve their own PRs on GitHub).
